      "path": "packages/belay/Belay/Sqlite/Programs/Claims/KeptReadCheck.lean",
      "rule": "R2",
      "severity": "must",
      "text": "The restatement of the public theorem `preserves_of_check` says only \"proves preservation of the read\", and drops that the conclusion holds for every database with the catalog of the schema, that it requires nothing when the migration fails, and that the bindings of the reference script are ignored. Fix: restate the conclusion with its `HasCatalog` precondition, the success domain and the ignored bindings, and restore a usage example such as `preserves_of_check (name! \"sessions\") allSessions (by decide +kernel)`."
    },
    {
      "id": "20261124T132211Z-304e4e02#2",
      "line": 20,
      "path": "packages/belay/Belay/Sqlite/Schema/Dispatch/ReadCertificate.lean",
      "rule": "R2",
      "severity": "should",
      "text": "The restatement of `ReadCertificate.Obligations` omits that the name finds the original table at the position of the certificate, and that the scan on the other connection is of the certificate moved to the other position. Fix: add one item for each conjunct of `appended`, including the two `tableNamed` lookups and the moved certificate."
    },
    {
      "id": "20261124T132211Z-304e4e02#3",
      "line": 37,
      "path": "packages/belay/Belay/Sqlite/Programs/Claims/KeptReadCheck.lean",
      "rule": "R11",
      "severity": "should",
      "text": "The new `reference` argument and `same` premise of `preserves_of_check` are used only with the read as its own reference (`RebuildKeeps.lean`, `KeptRead.original_preserves_of_check`), so no test would fail if the step-agreement transport were wrong or removed. Fix: add a surface test that applies `preserves_of_check` with a reference script that differs from the read and proves `same` explicitly."
    }
